## 3. Why This Design?

Zig forces you to confront the stack/heap distinction because it refuses to hide allocation. In languages with garbage collection, all objects are heap-allocated (or escape-analyzed) and the runtime manages stack references transparently. In C++, RAII with constructors/destructors can allocate on the heap inside a constructor without the caller being syntactically aware. Zig believes that *allocation is a side effect that must be visible and intentional*.

Why not provide a built-in `ArrayList` that allocates internally via a global allocator? Because that would hide control flow (allocations can fail), hide memory lifetime (who frees the list? when?), and prevent you from using the list in a `no_std` environment or with a custom allocator. By requiring an `Allocator` parameter, Zig makes the storage policy explicit and testable.

The stack is the default. Zig's syntax and standard library encourage stack allocation whenever possible. The compiler does not try to move things to the heap for you, so you never encounter surprises where a "local variable" causes an OOM because the runtime decided to box it. This gives the programmer absolute control and aligns with systems programming needs: you know exactly what will fit in L1 cache and what will trigger a page fault.

