## 6. Performance Considerations

### Stack Allocation: Zero-Cost Abstraction

Allocating on the stack involves nothing more than moving the stack pointer. On x86_64, `sub rsp, 0x1000` allocates 4096 bytes in one instruction. No branches, no locks, no cache misses (except potentially for the stack cache lines that will be touched). Access patterns exhibit excellent spatial and temporal locality because the stack frame is contiguous and frequently accessed. The CPU’s return stack buffer predicts function returns, keeping the pipeline full.

However, very large stack frames (> page size) may cause the OS to fault in guard pages, leading to a minor page fault. This is a one-time cost, but repeated large-stack calls in recursion can accumulate.

### Heap Allocation: Latency and Fragmentation

A heap allocation typically involves a library call that traverses a data structure to find a free block. `malloc` in glibc uses per-thread arenas and freelists; it’s fast on average but suffers from lock contention in multithreaded programs (though modern allocators reduce this). `GeneralPurposeAllocator` in Zig uses thread-local caches and bucketed sizes, providing amortized O(1) performance with low contention. However, worst-case allocation can involve `sbrk`/`mmap`, which enters the kernel and possibly zeroes memory. The performance is unpredictable compared to stack allocation.

Deallocation can be even more costly: `free` might merge adjacent free blocks and update freelists. With `ArenaAllocator`, deallocation is deferred to the entire arena destruction, so individual `free` calls are no-ops, drastically improving throughput at the cost of peak memory usage.

### `FixedBufferAllocator`: The Sweet Spot

The bump-pointer approach of `FixedBufferAllocator` is almost as fast as raw stack allocation. It is a thread-local, bounded, non-moving allocator with allocation costing a few instructions. It shines in scenarios where you need many small allocations with a known lifetime (e.g., processing an HTTP request). The downside is that you cannot free individual items out of order; if you need fine-grained deallocation, you’ll need a more sophisticated allocator (or store free lists manually). But for many patterns—parsing, building temporary data structures, string formatting—it eliminates the overhead of the general-purpose heap entirely.

### Cache Locality

Data allocated on the stack is tightly packed with other local variables, leading to high cache density. Heap allocations scatter data across memory unless you use a slab or arena that places related allocations contiguously. A `FixedBufferAllocator` creates a contiguous region of related objects, which is cache-friendly. Conversely, a `page_allocator` may return far-apart pages, causing TLB pressure and cache misses.

### Avoiding the Allocator Vtable Overhead

Calling `allocator.alloc()` involves an indirect call. In hot loops, this overhead can be significant. Zig encourages using `allocator.allocAdvanced` and typed allocation functions that often inline. You can also pass a comptime-known allocator type and use `@fieldParentPtr` tricks to monomorphize, but typically the vtable indirection is acceptable. If you are writing a function that should be alloc-free, don't take an allocator at all; require a pre-allocated buffer or a `*FixedBufferAllocator` directly.

