## 8. Examples

### Example 1: HTTP Request Parser Using Only the Stack

This example parses a fixed-size header buffer without any heap allocation. It uses `std.fmt.bufPrint` and `std.mem.split` to extract fields.

```zig
const std = @import("std");

fn parseContentLength(buf: [*]const u8, len: usize) !u32 {
    // Search in-place
    const header = "Content-Length: ";
    const start = std.mem.indexOf(u8, buf[0..len], header) orelse return error.MissingHeader;
    const val_start = start + header.len;
    const val_end = std.mem.indexOfScalar(u8, buf[val_start..len], '\r') orelse len;
    const val = buf[val_start .. val_start + val_end];
    return std.fmt.parseInt(u32, val, 10);
}

pub fn main() !void {
    const request = "GET / HTTP/1.1\r\nContent-Length: 42\r\n\r\n";
    // All stack-allocated, no heap.
    var buf: [128]u8 = undefined;
    @memcpy(buf[0..request.len], request);
    const len = request.len;

    const cl = try parseContentLength(&buf, len);
    std.debug.print("Content-Length: {}\n", .{cl});
}
```

All data is on the stack. No allocator parameter needed.

### Example 2: Stack-Backed `std.ArrayList` for Building a Response

Here we construct a dynamic string using `ArrayList`, but backed entirely by a FixedBufferAllocator on the stack. This eliminates heap allocations while providing the convenience of dynamic resizing up to the buffer limit.

```zig
const std = @import("std");

fn buildResponse(arena_buf: []u8, name: []const u8) ![]const u8 {
    var fba = std.heap.FixedBufferAllocator.init(arena_buf);
    const ally = fba.allocator();

    var list = std.ArrayList(u8).init(ally);
    // defer list.deinit(); not necessary in pure FBA but good for hygiene.
    errdefer list.deinit();

    try list.appendSlice("Hello, ");
    try list.appendSlice(name);
    try list.appendSlice("!");

    return list.toOwnedSlice(); // Returns a slice into the arena.
}

test "stack-backed ArrayList" {
    var buf: [256]u8 = undefined;
    const greeting = try buildResponse(&buf, "World");
    try std.testing.expectEqualStrings("Hello, World!", greeting);
    // buf is still valid; greeting points inside buf.
}
```

### Example 3: Alloc-Free Function with Caller-Provided Output Buffer

A typical pattern for parsers or serializers that want to be embeddable in any context (freestanding, WASM, OS kernel). The function never touches the heap.

```zig
fn intToAsciiHex(val: u32, out: []u8) ![]const u8 {
    const hex_chars = "0123456789ABCDEF";
    var idx: usize = out.len;
    var v = val;
    if (val == 0) {
        out[0] = '0';
        return out[0..1];
    }
    while (v != 0) : (v >>= 4) {
        idx -= 1;
        if (idx == 0) return error.NoSpaceLeft;
        out[idx] = hex_chars[v & 0xF];
    }
    return out[idx..];
}

test "intToAsciiHex" {
    var buf: [8]u8 = undefined;
    const result = try intToAsciiHex(0x1A3F, &buf);
    try std.testing.expectEqualStrings("1A3F", result);
}
```

The function doesn’t even accept an allocator—it works on raw stack memory.

